1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:02,575
It’s an hour before kickoff.

2
00:00:03,495 --> 00:00:08,129
Here’s the matchday workflow we’re building for PitchTrace 3D,

3
00:00:08,129 --> 00:00:10,840
starting with the first camera on the touchline.

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,550
Secure one camera to each fixed mount and label them A through D.

5
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:19,970
Lock the position and aim of each camera.

6
00:00:19,970 --> 00:00:23,716
Have a player take a ball from halfway to the far penalty area.

7
00:00:24,016 --> 00:00:26,628
Look at two fixed views side by side.

8
00:00:26,958 --> 00:00:29,686
Can you still pick out the ball and the shirt number?

9
00:00:30,066 --> 00:00:32,807
If a player blocks the ball in camera A,

10
00:00:32,807 --> 00:00:35,590
check the same instant in camera B.

11
00:00:35,590 --> 00:00:38,073
Both cameras stay fixed throughout the match.

12
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:44,319
Next, make sure the cameras expose their frames at the same instant.

13
00:00:45,111 --> 00:00:47,904
In this setup, they share a hardware trigger.

14
00:00:48,696 --> 00:00:51,449
Fire a flash where the cameras can see it,

15
00:00:51,449 --> 00:00:54,106
then mark the flash in each recording.

16
00:00:54,898 --> 00:00:57,374
Here’s the two-camera timing report.

17
00:00:58,166 --> 00:01:01,277
It shows how far apart the exposures were,

18
00:01:01,277 --> 00:01:03,808
and whether either camera missed a flash.

19
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,706
Now give every camera the same map of the pitch.

20
00:01:09,626 --> 00:01:13,639
Start with a calibration board to measure lens distortion.

21
00:01:14,559 --> 00:01:16,575
Then match visible pitch markings

22
00:01:16,575 --> 00:01:19,132
to points you’ve measured on the ground.

23
00:01:20,052 --> 00:01:24,069
A pole of known height gives you a reference above the grass.

24
00:01:24,989 --> 00:01:27,931
Finally, check a point you left out of calibration

25
00:01:27,931 --> 00:01:31,436
and see how closely its position lines up.

26
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:36,294
Before kickoff, assign the match ID,

27
00:01:36,294 --> 00:01:38,116
select the pitch and team sheets,

28
00:01:38,116 --> 00:01:40,585
and save the calibration for this setup.

29
00:01:40,885 --> 00:01:44,409
Check all four recordings and the available disk space.

30
00:01:44,709 --> 00:01:46,531
At the whistle, mark kickoff.

31
00:01:46,831 --> 00:01:50,588
In the planned system, the pitch-side Mac reconstructs the play

32
00:01:50,588 --> 00:01:53,363
and sends match updates to the Live gateway.

33
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,938
You can try the broadcast side today.

34
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:00,532
Start the local gateway, open the viewer,

35
00:02:00,532 --> 00:02:03,215
select Live, and choose Local sample.

36
00:02:03,743 --> 00:02:06,903
As the first update arrives, the players appear

37
00:02:06,903 --> 00:02:09,261
and the match clock starts moving.

38
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,658
On the viewer’s screen, the ball is already in play.

39
00:02:14,578 --> 00:02:17,865
Freeze the action and move the virtual camera around it.

40
00:02:18,065 --> 00:02:20,722
The players stay exactly where they were,

41
00:02:20,722 --> 00:02:23,777
so you can see who was available for the pass.

42
00:02:24,697 --> 00:02:27,760
Select Resume live to catch up with the match.

43
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:32,790
Here’s a goal from the built-in match.

44
00:02:33,535 --> 00:02:35,114
Nine puts it away.

45
00:02:35,859 --> 00:02:39,392
Now wind back to the tackle and watch from above.

46
00:02:40,136 --> 00:02:42,989
Keep your eye on nine, across from the ball.

47
00:02:45,363 --> 00:02:49,450
He starts his run while the defender is still dealing with seven.

48
00:02:50,195 --> 00:02:53,926
Play it at half speed and watch where that run takes him.

49
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:57,576
Remember the flash test before kickoff?

50
00:02:57,876 --> 00:02:59,708
Here’s what that timing gives you.

51
00:03:00,008 --> 00:03:03,618
In this two-camera test archive, pause at twenty-five seconds.

52
00:03:03,918 --> 00:03:07,828
Open Footage review and switch from camera A to camera B.

53
00:03:08,128 --> 00:03:11,007
You’re looking at the same instant from both positions.

54
00:03:11,307 --> 00:03:14,294
Try the 3D viewer yourself through the link below.
